  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates how extracellular acidity can oppositely affect the cellular uptake of weakly basic versus weakly acidic drugs. In both examples, only the uncharged form appears to move readily across the cell membrane, while protonated or deprotonated forms are less membrane-permeable.\n\nIn the upper schematic, a weak base outside the cell is surrounded by abundant H+, so more of the drug becomes protonated. Because this charged form seems to cross the membrane poorly, less drug enters the cell, indicated by the decrease in intracellular drug concentration. This represents reduced uptake of weak bases in an acidic environment.\n\nIn the lower schematic, the opposite pattern is shown for a weak acid. In the acidic extracellular space, the weak acid is shifted toward its neutral form, which appears better able to diffuse into the cell. As a result, intracellular drug concentration increases, as indicated by the upward arrow.\n\nOverall, the image conveys an ion-trapping or pH-partitioning concept: low extracellular pH can hinder accumulation of weak-base drugs but enhance accumulation of weak-acid drugs, simply by changing their ionization state and therefore their ability to cross membranes.",
